# 1. Distributed web infrastructure

Website: www.foobar.com
The `www` record (A record) points to the IP of the load balancer.

## Infrastructure

- 1 load balancer (HAProxy)
- 2 application servers, each containing:
  - 1 web server (Nginx)
  - 1 application server
  - 1 set of application files (the same code base on both servers)
  - 1 database (MySQL)
- The MySQL database of Server 1 is the Primary node, the MySQL database of
  Server 2 is the Replica node (Primary-Replica cluster).

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The browser asks the DNS for the IP of www.foobar.com and gets the IP of
   the load balancer.
2. The browser sends an HTTPS request to the load balancer.
3. HAProxy chooses one of the two servers and forwards the request to its
   Nginx web server.
4. Nginx serves static files itself, or forwards the request to the
   application server.
5. The application server runs the code base and queries the database
   (writes go to the Primary, reads can go to the Replica).
6. The response goes back through Nginx and HAProxy to the user.

## Why each additional element is added

- **Second server:** to remove the single point of failure of the previous
  design and to double the capacity. If one server fails or is being
  maintained, the other one can still answer the requests. The same code base
  is deployed on both servers.
- **Load balancer (HAProxy):** to distribute the incoming traffic between the
  two servers so none of them is overloaded. It also checks the health of the
  servers and stops sending traffic to a server that is down. It gives users
  one single entry point (one IP) whatever the number of servers behind it.
- **Database replica (MySQL Replica on Server 2):** to keep a live copy of the
  data on another machine. It protects against data loss, can serve read
  requests to reduce the load on the Primary, and can be promoted to Primary
  if the Primary fails.

## Load balancer distribution algorithm

The load balancer is configured with the **Round Robin** algorithm. It sends
each new request to the next server of the list, in order, and starts again
from the first one at the end of the list: request 1 goes to Server 1,
request 2 to Server 2, request 3 to Server 1, and so on. Each server receives
the same number of requests. (Weights can be added to give more requests to a
more powerful server.)

## Active-Active or Active-Passive

This load balancer enables an **Active-Active** setup: both servers are
active at the same time and both answer user requests.

- **Active-Active:** all nodes handle traffic at the same time. The load is
  shared, resources are fully used, and if one node fails the others take its
  traffic (with less total capacity).
- **Active-Passive:** only one node (the active one) handles the traffic. The
  other node (the passive one) waits on standby and is not used until the
  active node fails, then it takes over. Resources of the passive node are
  idle, but the setup is simpler.

## How a database Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works

The Primary node is the main database: it receives all the write operations
(INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE). It records every change in a log (the binary log).
The Replica node connects to the Primary, reads this log and applies the same
changes on its own copy of the data, so it stays synchronized with the
Primary (usually with a small delay). The Replica is read-only and can
answer read queries. If the Primary fails, the Replica has a copy of the data
and can be promoted to become the new Primary.

## Difference between the Primary node and the Replica node for the application

- The **Primary node** is the one the application uses for write operations
  (and for reads that must be perfectly up to date). It is the source of
  truth.
- The **Replica node** is used by the application only for read operations
  (SELECT). The application cannot write on it. Because of the replication
  delay, the data on the Replica can be slightly behind the Primary.

## Issues with this infrastructure

- **SPOF (Single Point Of Failure):**
  - The load balancer: there is only one. If it fails, nobody can reach the
    website even if both servers are healthy.
  - The Primary database: if it fails, the application can no longer write
    data, and the Replica is not promoted automatically.
  - Also, the Primary-Replica setup is not a full redundancy for writes: all
    writes depend on one node.
- **Security issues:**
  - No firewall: the servers and their open ports are exposed to the whole
    Internet, so attackers can try to reach services that should be private
    (like MySQL).
  - No HTTPS: the traffic between the users and the website is not
    encrypted, so it can be read or modified by a third party (a "man in the
    middle"). An SSL certificate is needed (for example terminated at the
    load balancer).
- **No monitoring:** we cannot know if a server, a service or the database is
  down, overloaded or slow. There is no data about CPU, memory, disk, QPS or
  errors, and nobody is alerted when something goes wrong, so problems are
  discovered by the users.
